REGIONAL REGULATION OF PAYAKUMBUH CITY

NUMBER 11 OF 2016

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF PAYAKUMBUH,

Considering:

a. that the Unitary State of the Republic of Indonesia guarantees the welfare of every citizen, including the protection of Women and Children from all forms of violence and discrimination as mandated in the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that the rise of crimes and violence against women and children in society requires increased commitment and coordination from the Government, Regional Government and Society as well as all stakeholders in order to take handling and prevention measures;
c. that the fulfillment of constitutional rights of women and children as well as the improvement of the quality of life of women and children is one of the mandatory affairs that becomes the duty, authority and responsibility of the Regional Government;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children.

In View of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number B of 1956 concerning the Establishment of Small City Autonomous Regions Within the Central Sumatra Provincial Region jo. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 8 of 1970 concerning the Implementation of the Government of Solok and Payakumbuh Municipalities (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1956 Number 19);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning the ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of all forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 3277);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning amendments to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
6. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3720);
8. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the second amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 Concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
9. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2008 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 64); 10. Government Regulation Number 65 of 2015 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Diversion and Handling of Children Under 12 (twelve) Years of Age (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 194, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5732);
11. Regional Regulation of Payakumbuh City Number 04 of 2008 Concerning Organization and Working Procedures of the Inspectorate and Technical Institutions within the Payakumbuh City Government (Regional Gazette of Payakumbuh City of 2008 Number 04);
12. Regional Regulation of Payakumbuh City Number 3 of 2013 Concerning the Civil Service Police Unit (Regional Gazette of Payakumbuh City of 2013 Number 03);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES

and

MAYOR OF PAYAKUMBUH

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING WOMEN AND CHILDREN

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES, OBJECTIVES AND SCOPE

Article 2

1. The implementation of protection of women is based on the principles of:
a. Best interests of women;
b. Gender justice and gender equality;
c. Local wisdom;
d. Legal Certainty;
3. The implementation of protection of children is based on the principles of:
a. Non-Discrimination;
b. Best interests of the child;
c. Right to life, survival and development;
d. Respect for the views of the child;
e. Legal Certainty.

Article 4

The scope of regulation of protection of women and children includes:
a. Prevention;
b. Implementation of protection of women and children;
c. Handling;
d. Empowerment, and
e. Guidance and supervision;

Article 5

Every woman has the right to:
a. Obtain protection from acts of discrimination;
b. Obtain sexual protection;
c. Obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence and neglect;
d. Obtain decent work according to her ability;
e. Obtain work leave while receiving wages/salary in accordance with statutory regulations;
f. Obtain education and teaching at all types of levels and pathways of education;
g. Obtain special protection in the performance of work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and or health in connection with women's reproductive function, and
h. Ownership and management of joint property in marriage in accordance with statutory regulations.

Article 6

Every child has the right:
a. To live, grow, develop and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity;
b. To obtain protection from acts of discrimination;
c. To obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
d. To obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence and neglect;
e. To a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
f. To obtain a birth certificate free of charge;
g. To embrace religion and worship according to the teachings of their religion with the guidance of parents/guardians;
h. Not to be forced to change religion by foster parents, adoptive parents and by anyone;
i. To be recognized and know their parents, to be raised and cared for by their own parents;
j. To obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual and social needs;
k. To obtain education and teaching in accordance with their interests and talents;
1. To obtain care, education and training, to improve themselves and the ability to participate in community and state life for children with special needs;
m. To express and have their opinions heard; and
n. To rest and utilize leisure time for self-development.

Part One

Local Government Article 7 (1) The Local Government in efforts to protect women is responsible for:
a. Guaranteeing women's rights without discrimination in accordance with laws and regulations;
b. Establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women's rights;
c. Cooperating with the community and/or community organizations in the implementation of women's protection in accordance with laws and regulations;
d. Conducting rehabilitation and empowerment of women who are victims of violence;
e. Providing special protection to women victims of violence and human trafficking, and
f. Taking preventive actions in order to prevent violations of women's rights.
2. The responsibilities of the Local Government in efforts to protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by SKPD in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 8

1. The Local Government in efforts to protect children is responsible for:
a. Guaranteeing fundamental rights of children without discrimination in accordance with laws and regulations;
b. Providing support for facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of child protection;
c. Guaranteeing the right of children to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the child;
d. Establishing and implementing policies and programs/activities for the protection of children's rights;
e. Cooperating with the community and/or community organizations in the implementation of child protection in accordance with laws and regulations;
f. Conducting rehabilitation of children who are victims of violence;
g. Providing special protection to children in emergency situations, children in conflict with the law, children victims of violence and human trafficking, children from minority and isolated groups, children exploited economically and/or sexually, children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic abuse and children with HIV/AIDS;
h. Organizing inclusive education for children with special needs, and i. Taking preventive actions in order to prevent violations of children's rights.
2. The responsibilities of the Local Government in efforts to protect children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by SKPD in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 12

1. Parents in efforts to protect children's rights are responsible for:
a. Caring for, maintaining, educating and protecting children;
b. Developing children in accordance with their abilities, talents and interests;
c. Providing formal and non-formal education;
d. Guiding children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and
e. Preventing marriage at child age.
2. In the event that parents do not exist, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a certain reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

Article 13

1. In order to prevent violations of women's rights, the Local Government:
a. Conducts socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. Provides guidelines on handling acts of violence against women;
c. Involves community participation in recovery and social reintegration;
d. Develops an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women;
e. Develops family empowerment programs;
f. Strengthens the function of community organizations working in the field of women's empowerment; and
g. Provides public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women.
2. In order to prevent violations of children's rights, the Local Government:
a. Makes efforts to prevent acts of violence against children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. Develops child-friendly schools;
c. Encourages increased participation of children through the establishment of children's forums;
d. Provides guidelines on handling acts of violence against children;
e. Involves community participation in recovery services and reintegration of child victims of violence.
f. Develops an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against children;
g. Strengthens the function of community organizations working in the field of child protection; and
h. Realizes the development of Child-Friendly City Realizes Child-Friendly Open Public Spaces (RPTRA).

Article 20

1. Every person is prohibited from employing and involving women in bad work;
2. Bad work as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. All work in the form of slavery or the like;b. All work that exploits, provides or offers women for prostitution, pornography production, pornographic performances or gambling.
c. All work that exploits, provides, or involves women for the production and trade of alcoholic beverages, narcotics, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances and/or
d. All work that endangers the health, safety and morals of women,
3. Employers who employ women are required to maintain decency, safety and security while at the workplace;
4. Employers are required to place female workers in places that are safe for women's reproductive health;
5. Employers are required to fulfill the rights of female workers to receive wages in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
6. Employers are required to provide special breastfeeding facilities at their enterprises.

Article 31

Children requiring special protection are protected in their right to obtain education, namely:
a. Children in emergency situations;
b. Children in conflict with the law;
c. Children from minority and isolated groups;
d. Children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. Children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances abuse;
f. Children who are victims of pornography;
g. Children with HIV/AIDS;
h. Child victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. Child victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. Child victims of sexual crimes;
k. Child victims of terrorism networks;
l. Children with Disabilities;
m. Child victims of maltreatment and neglect;n. Children with deviant social behavior;
o. Children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their Parents' condition.

Article 33

1. The provisions as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (1) may be exempted for those aged between 13 (thirteen) years to 15 (fifteen) years, to perform light work as long as it does not interfere with physical, mental and social development and health.
2. Employers who employ children in light work as referred to in paragraph (1) must meet the requirements:
a. Written permission from parents or guardian;
b. Work agreement between the employer and parents or guardian;
c. Maximum working time of 3 (three) hours;
d. Conducted during the day and does not interfere with school time;
e. Safety and health;
f. Clear employment relationship;
g. Receive wages in accordance with the provisions of applicable laws and regulations.
3. The provisions as referred to in paragraph (2) letter a, letter b, letter f and letter g are exempted for children who work in their family business.

Article 43

1. The implementation of handling women and children victims of violence is carried out through integrated services;
2. Integrated services for women and children victims of violence use the principles of:
a. Gender responsive;
b. Non-discrimination;
c. Equal and respectful relationships;
d. Maintaining confidentiality;
e. Providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. Respecting individual differences;
g. Non-judgmental;
h. Respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. Sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of appropriate language understood by victims;
j. Fast and simple;
k. Empathy, and
l. Fulfillment of the rights of women and children.

Article 47

1. Counseling services as referred to in Article 46 paragraph (2) aim to:
a. Assist victims in recognizing problems and finding effective ways to overcome them independently;
b. Empower victims to determine their own future;
c. Provide moral support for victims in facing the process they are undergoing, and
d. Make victims feel accepted in the community environment.
2. Counseling services are carried out by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of:
a. Counselors;
b. Psychologists, and
c. Psychiatrists.
3. Counseling services are carried out in places that guarantee a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from victims.
4. Stages of counseling services include:
a. Initial counseling;
b. Follow-up counseling;
c. Case conference; and
d. Home visit
